def e(a,c,r,s):edit(m,a,c,r,s)
def f(i,k,c,r,s,o=None):qe(q,'io-uring-q'+str(i),k,c,r,s,o)
e('why it beats epoll plus read','how it can reduce per-operation overhead','No unconditional faster premise.',u)
e('epoll solved the C10K problem, but it left two costs on the table. Every read still takes at least two system calls (wait, then read), and it doesn\'t work for regular files at all. Linux\'s earlier native AIO (`io_submit`) handled files only with `O_DIRECT` and was widely considered a dead end.','epoll reduces repeated interest-set scanning, but applications still perform I/O after readiness notifications. Waits can cover many reads, so the cost is not invariably two syscalls per read. Ordinary regular files do not provide useful storage-completion readiness through epoll. Linux native AIO predates io_uring but has operation/filesystem limitations; O_DIRECT is not a universal requirement or a guarantee of non-blocking submission.','Fix syscall-count and AIO restrictions.',u)
e('most I/O-related system calls.','a growing, version-dependent set of I/O operations.','Avoid unsourced coverage quantifier.',u)
e('64 bytes each','normally 64 bytes; optional extended formats exist','SQE128 exception.',st)
e('16 bytes each','normally 16 bytes; extended formats also exist','CQE32 exception.',st)
e('With a single producer and single consumer per ring, they need no locks, just memory barriers so each side sees the other\'s writes in the right order.','In a single-user-producer/consumer design, index publication uses the required memory ordering rather than a userspace mutex. Multiple application threads must coordinate ring access, and kernel completion producers require their own synchronization; this is not a claim that the entire subsystem is lock-free.','Logical role notone executionthread.',u)
e('Almost nobody uses these directly.','Applications commonly use the **liburing** wrappers; direct use requires following the ABI and memory-ordering rules.','Remove unsupported prevalence. ',u)
e('The **liburing** library wraps them:','A minimal single-request liburing fragment follows (supply fd, a 4096-byte buffer, request data and error callbacks):','Codefragment setup.',sub)
e('io_uring_queue_init(256, &ring, 0);','int ret = io_uring_queue_init(256, &ring, 0);\nif (ret < 0) {\n    fail(-ret); exit(1);\n}','Check negative error directly.',sub)
e('    io_uring_get_sqe(&ring);','    io_uring_get_sqe(&ring);\nif (!sqe) { fail(ENOSPC); exit(1); }','SQE acquisition canfail.',sub)
e('io_uring_submit(&ring);','ret = io_uring_submit(&ring);\nif (ret != 1) {\n    fail(ret < 0 ? -ret : EIO); exit(1);\n}','Expectedone request submission failure handling.',sub)
e('io_uring_wait_cqe(&ring, &cqe);','do {\n    ret = io_uring_wait_cqe(&ring, &cqe);\n} while (ret == -EINTR);\nif (ret < 0) { fail(-ret); exit(1); }','Neverdereference uninitialized CQE onerror.',sub)
e('io_uring_cqe_seen(&ring, cqe);','io_uring_cqe_seen(&ring, cqe);\nio_uring_queue_exit(&ring);','Examplecleanup.',sub)
e('## Why it\'s faster','## Where overhead can be reduced','Conditionalperformance.',st)
e('Syscalls got noticeably more expensive after the 2018 Spectre and Meltdown mitigations (page-table isolation and friends), which made batching more valuable.','Batching amortises syscall overhead. Mitigations, CPU architecture and workload affect the size of the saving; this lesson supplies no universal measured syscall-cost increase.','Unmeasured ranking andmitigation effect.',st)
e('### 2. SQPOLL: no syscalls at all','### 2. SQPOLL: fewer submission syscalls','Steadystatecapability notallsetupwaitsyscalls.',st)
e('Every syscall on an fd normally looks it up in the process\'s file table and takes a reference, then drops it. For I/O into user memory, the kernel must also pin the pages each time.','Ordinary descriptor resolution and resource management have costs, though optimized paths can avoid some reference work. Page pinning is required by some I/O paths, not every buffered copy into user memory.','No everyreadpins/userfdref universal.',reg)
e('no per-op lookup and refcounting.','avoid ordinary per-operation descriptor-table resolution while retaining registration/resource bookkeeping.','Fixedtable still lookup/bookkeeping.',reg)
e('### 4. Real async for files','### 4. Completion interfaces for files','Threadbasedcompletion alsoasync.',u)
e('For each request, the kernel first tries the operation **inline** without blocking. If it would block:','Execution depends on opcode, flags and kernel/filesystem support. One common path tries the operation **inline**, then defers if needed:','IOSQE_ASYNCandotherpaths exceptions.',u)
e('No thread involved.','This can avoid dedicating a blocked worker to the wait; threads still execute submission and completion work.','No threads statementliteralfalse.',st)
e('This fixes the idle-connection memory problem of completion I/O (lesson 2).','This can reduce dedicated per-connection buffer retention, but the shared pool still consumes memory and needs capacity/recycling controls.','Poolnotmemoryfree.',buf)
e('run "read then write" as a chain without returning to user space in between.','order supported operations without a userspace round trip; errors can cancel subsequent soft links. Linking does not automatically replace a write length with the preceding read\'s result.','Linkedreadshortlength pitfall.',u)
a=Path(m).read_text();stt=a.index('> [!example] Where it\'s used');ed=a.index('\n\n## Getting',stt)
e(a[stt:ed],'> [!example] Versioned integration examples\n> PostgreSQL 18 documents an optional `io_method = io_uring` backend when built with support. libuv documents `UV_LOOP_USE_IO_URING_SQPOLL`, added in 1.49.0, for supported asynchronous filesystem operations. Availability and defaults depend on the deployed runtime, build and platform; inspect those settings.','Retain directly verified examples; remove unverified product list/history.',['https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html','https://docs.libuv.org/en/v1.x/loop.html'])
a=Path(m).read_text();stt=a.index('io_uring is not magic.');ed=a.index('\n\n## Pitfalls',stt)
e(a[stt:ed],'> [!note] Performance evidence gap\n> No reproducible benchmark accompanies the original percentage gaps, per-core IOPS figures or workload ranking table. Those claims are omitted. Compare the actual kernel, operation mix, queue depth, CPU budget and storage/network configuration against a measured baseline; batching or polling can help some workloads and hurt others.','Removeunsupported exactperformance andtable.',st)
e('**Buffer lifetime.** The kernel may write into your buffer at any moment until the CQE arrives. Freeing or reusing it early corrupts memory.','**Buffer lifetime.** Keep ordinary read/receive buffers valid until the matching operation completes. For zero-copy send, the first CQE may not release the buffer: if MORE is set, await the notification CQE. Multishot operations and provided-buffer ownership have their own rules.','SEND_ZC firstCQEdoesnotendbufferuse.',zc+ms)
e('**SQE contents are read at submit time**, but pointed-to data (buffers, `iovec` arrays on older kernels, paths) may be read later. Keep it all alive.','**SQE consumption and referenced data lifetimes differ.** Follow each opcode\'s rules and SUBMIT_STABLE feature behavior; SQPOLL submission return does not necessarily prove the kernel already consumed the SQE. Payload buffers generally live through operation completion.','Submissionstable/opcodescope.',sub+st)
e('Modern kernels hold overflowed CQEs in a backlog (`IORING_FEAT_NODROP`) rather than dropping them, but it costs memory and performance.','With IORING_FEAT_NODROP, the kernel normally backlogs overflowed CQEs, at a memory/performance cost. Allocation failure can still lose events; NODROP is not an absolute no-loss guarantee.','Documented OOMexception.',st)
e('Google disabled io_uring on ChromeOS and on its production servers, and blocks it for Android apps with seccomp.','The same June 2023 post described then-current restrictions on ChromeOS, production servers and Android apps. This is historical reporting, not proof of every current Google deployment policy.','Historicaldatedpolicy.',gg)
e('**Docker\'s** default seccomp profile blocks the io_uring syscalls, so containers can\'t use it unless you opt in.','The inspected upstream **Moby default seccomp profile** uses an allowlist that omits io_uring syscalls, denying them under that profile. Actual Docker/container policy depends on version, capabilities and the selected profile.','Sourcecheckedprofile notallcontainers.',dk)
e('**Visibility.** Security tools that watch system calls (seccomp filters, many EDR and audit products) see only `io_uring_enter`, not the individual opens, reads and network sends performed through the ring. Researchers have demonstrated rootkits that do their I/O entirely through io_uring to evade syscall-based monitoring.','**Visibility.** A monitor limited to ordinary syscall-entry events does not automatically see one read/open/send syscall per SQE. It may see setup/enter/register calls, while SQPOLL can process submissions without an enter call. Other hooks, audit support and io_uring-aware instrumentation may observe operations; syscall-entry visibility alone is not a complete security model.','No universal allEDRblind/onlyenter/rootkitclaim.',u+st)
e('`2` disables it entirely.','`2` disables new ring creation for everyone. Existing rings remain usable under either restrictive value.','sysctldoesnotrevokeexistingrings.',sys)
e('lets a process lock down which opcodes a ring may use before handing it to less trusted code.','lets a process constrain opcodes/flags on a ring created with IORING_SETUP_R_DISABLED, before enabling and handing it to less trusted code. It is an allowlist, not a complete sandbox by itself.','Disabled-ring prerequisite.',reg)
e('Lock-free single-producer, single-consumer rings in shared memory mean submissions and completions involve no copying of request structures and few or no syscalls.','Shared rings can reduce submission/completion transfer overhead; correct ordering and concurrent-access coordination remain necessary. Payload copying is a separate issue.','No whollylockfree/nointernalcopyclaim.',u)
e('real async file I/O','completion-based file I/O','Threadbaseddoesnotfakeasync.',u)
e('buffers must stay valid until their CQE arrives.','buffer lifetime follows the operation, including zero-copy-send notification and multishot rules.','Recaplifetimeexception.',zc+ms)
e('It has a large attack surface and bypasses syscall-level monitoring, so Google, Docker and others restrict it; Linux 6.6+ can disable it with `kernel.io_uring_disabled`.','Historical vulnerabilities motivated deployment restrictions. Check actual container policy and instrumentation; kernel.io_uring_disabled controls new ring creation rather than revoking existing rings.','Recappolicyandrevocation.',sys+gg+dk)
f(1,'text','The kernel produces completion entries according to the operation and flags','NotoneCQEeveryop.',u,0)
f(1,'explanation','Correct. Normal CQEs are 16 bytes, with optional formats. Multishot operations can emit several CQEs, successful CQEs can sometimes be suppressed, and zero-copy sends can emit a later buffer-release notification.','Entryformatandcount exceptions.',st+zc+ms,0)
f(1,'explanation','The logical producer/consumer roles do not imply every execution context is a single thread. Application sharing requires synchronization, and the kernel coordinates completion production.','Roleversusthread.',u,3)
f(1,'workedExample','SQ: application publishes requests, kernel consumes them. CQ: kernel publishes results, application consumes them. Publication uses defined memory ordering. Coordinate multiple userspace threads rather than assuming the entire subsystem is lock-free. CQE count and size depend on opcode and setup flags.','Correctlockingmodel.',u+st)
f(2,'explanation','Per-operation failure is encoded directly in cqe->res. Many liburing APIs also return negative errno directly rather than setting the C errno variable; check the called function\'s contract.','Liburingerrorsnotglobalerrno.',sub,1)
f(3,'explanation','It is not a checksum. The application chooses a tag that is returned with completions; some operations such as cancellation can also use user_data as an identifier to match pending work.','Kernelcanmatchtag notneverinterpret.',u,1)
f(4,'prompt','In a simplified successful batch, one epoll_wait returns 100 readable sockets and each needs one read. For io_uring, assume 100 receives fit in the ring and one enter call submits and waits for all 100 completions without interruption. Excluding setup and retries, compare syscall counts.','Onecallallcompletions requiresconditions.',u)
f(4,'workedExample','1. Stated epoll path: one wait plus 100 reads = 101 syscalls.\n2. Stated io_uring path: publish 100 SQEs, then one io_uring_enter requesting submission and waiting for 100 completions.\n3. Reap the 100 CQEs from shared memory.\n4. Waiting for only one completion does not guarantee all 100 are ready; real paths may require more waits, handle partial submissions, interruptions or other progress work. This is a syscall-count model, not a benchmark.','Originalmincomplete1 didnotwaitall.',u)
f(5,'prompt','An SQPOLL ring has no pending work. After its poller exceeds the configured idle timeout and sleeps, an application publishes a new SQE but omits the documented wakeup check. Why may it remain unprocessed?','Explicitcausalmissingwake notguessfewsecs.',st)
f(5,'explanation','SQPOLL is submission polling, not the IOPOLL completion mode whose read/write paths require supported direct I/O. Opcode/setup restrictions still depend on kernel version.','Notanyoperationuniversal.',st,0)
f(6,'prompt','Which mechanisms can reduce io_uring overhead for suitable workloads and supported configurations? Select all that apply.','Notguaranteedspeedadvantage.',u)
f(6,'text','Registered files avoid ordinary per-operation descriptor-table resolution','Fixedfile stillindexlookup.',reg,1)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Registration retains file resources and permits indexed use, reducing ordinary descriptor resolution/reference overhead; it does not eliminate all bookkeeping.','Noallrefcountfalse.',reg,1)
f(6,'explanation','Correct for supported fixed-buffer operations. Registration can amortize pinning/mapping work; ordinary buffered copies do not universally pin pages per operation.','Comparisononlyapplicablepaths.',reg,2)
f(6,'workedExample','Batching can amortize submission costs; registered resources can amortize appropriate lookup/pinning costs; SQPOLL can remove steady-state submission syscalls when its thread remains active. These are mechanisms, not guaranteed speedups. An ordinary buffered read still copies payload, whereas direct I/O or specific zero-copy features follow different paths.','Avoidallreadsnecessarilycopy.',reg+st)
f(7,'prompt','Why can io_uring expose a completion interface for supported buffered regular-file reads when epoll readiness does not report their storage completion?','No trulyasynchierarchy.',u)
f(7,'explanation','Correct in principle. A completion request can finish inline, through supported asynchronous paths or through io-wq workers. The path varies with opcode, flags, filesystem and kernel version.','Notalwayspuntifblock.',u,3)
f(8,'prompt','Assume setup, SQE allocation and submission succeed and the receive remains pending. What does this fragment risk?\n\n```c\nchar buf[4096];\nsqe = io_uring_get_sqe(&ring);\nio_uring_prep_recv(sqe, fd,\n    buf, sizeof buf, 0);\nio_uring_submit(&ring);\nreturn;  /* buf goes out of scope */\n```','Setupsuccesstosinglequestionlifetime.',u)
f(8,'explanation','The request is pending in this scenario. Later access might corrupt reused stack memory or fail depending on mapping changes; immediate EFAULT is not a guaranteed safeguard.','Notalwaysvalidsubmitmeansnofault.',u,3)
f(9,'prompt','A server hypothetically retains one **16 KiB** buffer for each of 50,000 pending receives, totaling 781.25 MiB of buffer capacity. Which feature can reduce per-connection retention by drawing from a smaller shared pool?','Exactbinaryarithmetic andcapacitynotRSS.',buf)
f(9,'explanation','Correct. Provided buffers decouple dedicated buffer ownership from every idle receive. Size the pool for outstanding data and processing lifetime, and handle exhaustion/replenishment; pending receives do not inherently mean every byte is physically pinned.','Pinningandpoolsize notactiveconnectioncount.',buf,2)
f(9,'workedExample','1. 50,000 × 16 KiB = 819,200,000 bytes = 781.25 MiB of capacity, not necessarily the same RSS.\n2. An illustrative shared pool of 1,024 such buffers uses 16 MiB.\n3. Receive completion flags identify selected buffers. Recycle only after application processing and kernel-use rules allow it.\n4. The smaller pool can exhaust during bursts; handle ENOBUFS/termination behavior and replenish/rearm as the operation requires.','Poolmemoryisnotguaranteedfix.',buf+ms)
f(10,'prompt','Which statements about documented io_uring deployment restrictions and syscall-entry monitoring are accurate? Select all that apply.','Notassumeuserplatformpolicy.',dk+gg)
f(10,'text','The inspected upstream Moby default seccomp allowlist omits io_uring syscalls','Sourceexactprofile.',dk,0)
f(10,'explanation','Correct for that profile. Actual container policy depends on the selected runtime version/profile and configuration; changing the profile is not the only possible prerequisite.','Policy conditional.',dk,0)
f(10,'text','Monitoring only ordinary syscall-entry events does not automatically expose every SQE as a separate read/open/send syscall','Noalltoolsblind.',u,1)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. Ring operations require suitable instrumentation; setup/register/enter can be visible, and other hooks may observe operations. SQPOLL need not enter once per submission.','Instrumentation scope.',u+st,1)
f(10,'text','In its June 2023 post, Google reported about 60% of prior-year kCTF exploit submissions used io_uring','Historicaldatedstatistic.',gg,2)
f(10,'explanation','Correct as dated reporting. It does not establish the same proportion or deployment policy today.','Historicalscope.',gg,2)
f(10,'workedExample','1. Check the actual profile: the inspected Moby allowlist does not include io_uring setup/enter/register calls.\n2. A syscall-entry-only monitor does not automatically see one syscall for every ring operation; other instrumentation may.\n3. The 60% statistic belongs to Google\'s June 2023 historical report, not a current universal rate.\n4. Unprivileged ring creation is possible when policy permits; kernel settings and seccomp can restrict it.','No unqualifiedcurrentclaims.',dk+gg+sys)
f(11,'prompt','A ring reports IORING_FEAT_NODROP. Its fixed CQ is full while completions arrive; assume memory remains available for overflow bookkeeping and no explicit resize. What normally happens?','NODROPOOMexception andnewresizefeature.',st)
f(11,'explanation','Under ordinary overflow, the ring is not automatically enlarged. Applications must reap completions; explicit resize/configuration support is version-dependent.','Notforeverfixedallkernels.',st,1)
f(11,'explanation','Correct under the memory-available assumption. Overflow bookkeeping costs memory and may impose backpressure. Allocation failure can still drop events, so NODROP is not an absolute guarantee.','Remove nothinglostfalse.',st,3)
f(11,'workedExample','1. With default sizing, CQ capacity is twice SQ capacity; custom sizing is possible.\n2. NODROP normally stores completions internally while the CQ is full, then flushes them as space becomes available.\n3. Memory exhaustion can still cause loss and overflow/error indications. Older kernels can return EBUSY on submission when overflow cannot flush; exact behavior is version-dependent.\n4. Reap promptly and apply backpressure. A larger CQ adds headroom, not unlimited capacity.','NODROPbounded andversioned.',st)
